WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
The double-slit experiment&nbsp;
defies our understanding.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.680
It involves a laser shooting beams&nbsp;
of light particles, called photons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.200
at a screen with two parallel slits.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.500
If we mark all the spots where&nbsp;
the photons hit the second wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:17.640
we should expect to see two strips&nbsp;
to correspond with the two slits.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.320
But that’s not what we see.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.640
Instead, we see an alternating&nbsp;
pattern of light and dark bands.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:29.220
This is characteristic of the behavior of waves&nbsp;
that pass through both slits simultaneously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.880
interfering with each other to create&nbsp;
the pattern on the second screen.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.160
The bright lines are where the top of waves&nbsp;
meet, resulting in a more intense or brighter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.200
area on the screen. The darker bands are where&nbsp;
the top of one wave meets the bottom of another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.600
so they cancel each other out, resulting in&nbsp;
a less intense or darker area on the screen.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.260
Here is a photo of a real interference pattern.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.960
The original double-slit experiment was conducted&nbsp;
in 1801 by British polymath Thomas Young.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.320
Since then, it’s been performed&nbsp;
with various types of particles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.220
including electrons, and they were&nbsp;
found to behave in the same way.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:10.200
This phenomenon occurs even if the&nbsp;
photons are fired one at a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:16.920
which suggests that the photon is interfering with&nbsp;
itself as if it passes through both slits at once.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:22.440
Physicists were so stumped that they decided to&nbsp;
observe which slit the particle went through.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.960
And that’s when things get really weird.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:30.420
When scientists used a measuring device to&nbsp;
observe the slit that each photon passed through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:36.120
the interference pattern disappeared, and&nbsp;
the photons started behaving like particles.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:40.980
Instead of the spectrum of light and&nbsp;
dark bands, we see two bright bands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.340
indicating that the photons&nbsp;
chose one slit or the other.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.400
So light can display characteristics of both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.820
particles and waves, known&nbsp;
as wave-particle duality.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.540
It appears that light decides&nbsp;
to behave as a wave when it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.560
not being watched and acts like a&nbsp;
particle when it is being measured.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:04.260
The mere act of observing which slit it went&nbsp;
through changed the behavior of the photons!

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.520
Almost as if they were aware&nbsp;
they were being watched!

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.560
It’s kind of like how in a video game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.540
the environment and objects only load when&nbsp;
the player focuses on or interacts with them.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.420
The entire world isn't rendered all at once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.260
allowing the game to save processing&nbsp;
power and optimize resources.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:27.000
Likewise, light seems to behave&nbsp;
like waves, but when we observe it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.920
it's as if we're "loading" its properties,&nbsp;
causing it to change and act like particles.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.860
Physicist Richard Feynman famously said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:40.440
"the double-slit experiment is absolutely&nbsp;
impossible to explain in any classical way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:46.440
and has in it the heart of quantum mechanics.&nbsp;
In reality, it contains the only mystery."

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.620
The most widely accepted theory to explain this&nbsp;
phenomenon comes from physicist Niels Bohr,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:57.780
who proposed that particles like photons don’t&nbsp;
have definite properties until they’re observed.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:02.880
So in the case of light, photons exist&nbsp;
in a wave-like form representing a range&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.340
of possible positions until they are observed.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.800
When we measure or observe the particle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.640
we force it to “choose” a definite&nbsp;
state, behaving like particles.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.860
This is called the Copenhagen interpretation,&nbsp;
named in honor of Bohr’s home city.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:23.580
However, to this day, no one knows why observing&nbsp;
a particle causes its behavior to change.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:26.520
Not everyone was a fan of Bohr’s theory.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:31.800
Einstein disagreed that particles could be&nbsp;
in multiple states at once until observed.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:35.280
He was uncomfortable with the&nbsp;
inherent randomness of such an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:40.080
interpretation and famously said, “God&nbsp;
does not play dice with the universe.”

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.820
Einstein believed that the universe&nbsp;
followed a consistent set of rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:49.680
like how his general relativity theory&nbsp;
predicted the motion of planets around the sun.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.740
He spent his later years trying to develop a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.940
unified theory that explained everything&nbsp;
in the universe, but he didn’t succeed.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:01.800
Physicists recently conducted a&nbsp;
variation on the double-slit experiment.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:07.320
In April 2023, researchers at Imperial&nbsp;
College London swapped out the screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:12.960
with two slits for a transparent material&nbsp;
used in smartphone screens called ITO.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.020
They first fired a laser beam at the material.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:20.340
When a second laser was directed at&nbsp;
the material and pulsed super quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:25.680
in quadrillionths of a second, it caused the&nbsp;
material to become temporarily reflective.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:30.780
The interaction of Laser 2 with the&nbsp;
material changed the frequency - and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:35.280
therefore the color - of the light from&nbsp;
Laser 1 that was reflected off the material.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:41.400
This experiment shows that by manipulating time&nbsp;
rather than space, they were able to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:46.980
interference patterns similar to the interference&nbsp;
observed in the original double-slit experiment.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.160
There are certain things in&nbsp;
life we take for granted.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.280
If you don’t get enough sleep,&nbsp;
you’ll feel tired and groggy.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.760
If you drive recklessly, you are more&nbsp;
likely to get into a car accident.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:01.260
But sometimes, there are principles that&nbsp;
govern our lives that we just can’t explain.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:06.180
The perplexing result of the double-slit&nbsp;
experiment is up for interpretation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:10.680
with some suggestions that this is&nbsp;
evidence our world could be “programmed”.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:14.280
So far, not even the best scientific minds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:18.480
can explain the bizarre behavior&nbsp;
of particles…at least, not yet.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:22.080
There are many puzzles that are perplexing.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.560
For example, can you tell what’s&nbsp;
wrong with the gears Leonardo da&nbsp;&nbsp;

